    body: "Create an interactive simulation demonstrating the 'sunk cost fallacy' in a basketball context, inspired by the Detroit Pistons' historical draft decisions.\n\nFeatures:\n- Create a basketball court background with a vintage, slightly faded aesthetic, reminiscent of old arenas. The court should have a prominent Pistons logo at center court.\n- Display a 'Draft Pick' counter prominently, showing the number of draft picks invested.\n- Display a 'Player Performance' meter, visually representing the current player's effectiveness (e.g., a bar filling up, or a numerical rating). This meter should fluctuate over time.\n- Implement a 'Sunk Cost' visual: a growing pile of basketballs or draft pick cards accumulating in a corner of the screen, representing the resources already invested. This pile should visually increase with each 'invested' draft pick.\n- Create a 'Decision Panel' with two buttons: 'Continue Investing' and 'Cut Losses'.\n- The simulation should run in discrete 'game' or 'season' steps, with the player's performance randomly fluctuating each step.\n- When 'Continue Investing' is chosen, the 'Draft Pick' counter increases, and the 'Sunk Cost' visual grows. The player's performance continues to evolve.\n- When 'Cut Losses' is chosen, the simulation resets, and the 'Draft Pick' counter and 'Sunk Cost' visual return to zero.\n- Display a 'Fan Sentiment' meter (e.g., a crowd meter or a simple text indicator) that decreases as the 'Sunk Cost' increases and player performance remains low.\n- Use a color scheme of faded teal, red, and white, reflecting classic Pistons colors.\n\nUser Actions:\n1. Click the 'Continue Investing' button to simulate investing another draft pick into the current player, advancing the simulation by one step.\n2. Click the 'Cut Losses' button to abandon the current player and reset the simulation, starting fresh with a new 'draft pick'.\n3. Press the 'R' key to instantly reset the entire simulation, clearing all counters and visuals, as if starting a new season from scratch.\nNote:\n- Your output should be implemented in JavaScript with HTML and CSS.\n- Ensure that the output has both index.js and index.html files\n",
