      prompt:
        "Create an interactive molecular synthesis visualization that allows users to combine basic chemical elements into complex structures.\n\nFeatures:\n- Display a central 'reaction chamber' area where molecular structures are assembled. This chamber should have a subtle, pulsating glow.\n- Provide a palette of at least 5 basic 'element' nodes (e.g., Carbon, Hydrogen, Oxygen, Nitrogen, Phosphorus) represented by distinct colored circles with their chemical symbols. Each element should have a unique, vibrant color (e.g., Carbon: dark grey, Hydrogen: white, Oxygen: red, Nitrogen: blue, Phosphorus: orange).\n- When an element node is dragged into the reaction chamber, it should snap to a grid or a magnetic-like attraction point, allowing for easy alignment.\n- As element nodes are brought close together, a visual 'bond' should form between them, represented by a glowing line. The bond should dynamically adjust its length and angle as elements are moved.\n- Implement a 'bond strength' indicator that changes color based on the number of bonds an element has (e.g., green for 1-2 bonds, yellow for 3-4 bonds, red for 5+ bonds).\n- Allow for the creation of complex structures by connecting multiple elements. The connections should be visually distinct (e.g., single, double, triple bonds could be represented by different line thicknesses or patterns).\n- Include a 'molecular stability' meter that visually represents the complexity and stability of the current structure. This meter should increase as more stable bonds are formed and decrease if bonds are broken or elements are isolated.\n- Implement a 'reset' button that clears all elements and bonds from the reaction chamber with a smooth fade-out animation.\n- The background of the visualization should be a dark, deep blue, with subtle, shimmering particles to evoke a microscopic, chemical environment.\n\nUser Actions:\n1. Drag and drop element nodes from the palette into the reaction chamber to begin forming molecules. Elements should automatically form bonds when brought into proximity.\n2. Click and drag existing element nodes within the reaction chamber to rearrange them, breaking and reforming bonds dynamically.\n3. Click the 'reset' button to clear the reaction chamber and start a new synthesis.\nNote:\n- Your output should be implemented in JavaScript with HTML and CSS.\n- Ensure that the output has both index.js and index.html files\n",
